==============================================================================
  WHERE THE AMPLITUDE STANDS
==============================================================================

  A-A  precision needed per band to match the molecular bound
       sigma_eps = sigma / sqrt(sum w^2) = sigma / sqrt(8)
       to reach sigma_eps = 4.0e-07:  sigma = 1.131e-06
  ok    A-A1 the pair needs sigma ~ 1.1e-6 per band   1.131e-06

  A-B  how far the paper's own figure sits from it
       1.4e-05 / 4.0e-07 = 35.0
  ok    A-B1 the molecular bound is 35x tighter   35.0x

  A-C  direction of the inequality
  ok    A-C1 molecular really is the tighter of the two   so the paper cannot claim to improve the amplitude

  A-D  ANTI-CONTROL: does the conclusion depend on WHICH methanol number?
       with the superseded 1.1e-07:  sigma = 3.111e-07
  ok    A-D1 the superseded number would demand MORE precision, not less   so quoting the robust figure is the CONSERVATIVE choice against our own case
  ok    A-D2 the paper is beaten on either reading   the framing change is forced, not a judgement call

  A-D'  independent check: Bagdonaite et al. 2013, three telescopes, ten lines
       (-1.0 +/- 0.8_stat +/- 1.0_sys) e-7   combined 1 sigma = 1.281e-07
       worst 2-sigma excursion |dmu/mu| < 3.56e-07
  ok    A-D'1 the two independent analyses agree at the 4e-7 level   3.56e-07 vs 4.0e-07
  ok    A-D'2 so 4e-7 is not an underquote of the methanol constraint   two teams, two line sets, same answer
  ok    A-D'3 and it too is systematics-dominated, as the 21 cm row is   sys 1.0e-07 > stat 8.0e-08

  A-E  the assumption chains, counted
       21 cm  -> eps  needs: alpha fixed, proton g-factor fixed, m_p fixed
       CH3OH  -> eps  needs: m_p fixed
  ok    A-E1 the methanol chain is strictly shorter   1 assumption vs 3
  ok    A-E2 and strictly contained, so it is weaker and not merely different

  A-F  ANTI-CONTROL: is the paper's answer to all this actually non-empty?
       one row:  1 number - 1 fitted amplitude  =>  0 degree(s) of freedom left to test with
       two rows: 2 numbers - 1 fitted amplitude  =>  1 degree(s) of freedom left to test with
  ok    A-F1 a single-row bound tests NOTHING once the amplitude is fitted   any hypothesis absorbs it
  ok    A-F2 the pair leaves one testable degree of freedom   and that is the paper's actual contribution
  ok    A-F3 the discriminating power does NOT scale with the amplitude bound   it is a counting statement, independent of sigma -- so Finding 1 does not empty it

==============================================================================
  RESULT — THE FRAMING WAS WRONG AND IS NOW CORRECTED
==============================================================================

  The paper quoted |eps| < 1.4e-5 from the 21 cm row without knowing that methanol
  absorption toward PKS1830-211 already bounds the same quantity at 4e-7, under a
  STRICTLY CONTAINED set of assumptions -- methanol carries mu alone, where 21 cm
  carries g_p alpha^2 / mu and needs two more constants held still. The molecular limit
  is 35x tighter, and the 21 cm and Faraday pair would need 1.1e-6 fractional precision
  per band merely to match it.

  So the paper cannot claim to improve the amplitude, and the text no longer does. The
  abstract, the sensitivity section and the conclusion all now say so outright.

  THE ANSWER IS NOT EMPTY, and A-F is why. Once the amplitude is fitted, a single-row
  bound has zero degrees of freedom left -- every hypothesis absorbs it, so it tests
  nothing about WHERE the shift sits. Two rows of different weight leave one. That is a
  counting statement and does not scale with sigma, so a 35x better amplitude bound does
  not touch it. A tight mu limit and a weak pattern test answer different questions.

  Separately, the dispersion-measure row had a varying-constants literature the paper
  cited nowhere -- Lemos et al. 2025 (JCAP 01, 059) and Kalita 2024 (MNRASL 533, L57),
  both comparing observed DM against Omega_b h^2 with an assumed baryon fraction. That
  is precisely the external electron column the paper's own demotion argument named, so
  it CONFIRMS the reasoning. But Kalita's dmu/mu = -1e-5 is derived from his alpha
  result through an assumed unification relation, not measured independently, and so
  constrains nothing at fixed alpha. Both are now cited, with that distinction stated.

